\title{Separating Temporal Lag from Pitch Interval:\\ A Tropical Theory of Digit Melodies}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 23, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A recurring claim in the literature on ``digit music'' holds that a peak in the autocorrelation of a decimal digit sequence at lag $12$ evidences \emph{octave} structure in the corresponding melody. The claim conflates two independent variables: the \emph{temporal lag}, an element of the additive monoid $\N$ indexing which pairs of positions are compared, and the \emph{pitch interval}, an element of $\{0,\dots,b-1\}$ recording what is heard when a pair is compared. We develop the theory of both variables for base-$b$ digit melodies and prove that they are separated as completely as two variables can be. On the pitch side we introduce the lag-$\ell$ pitch-interval distribution and prove that it has total mass equal to the window length and support contained in $\{0,\dots,b-1\}$ --- so that in base ten the octave value has mass zero at every lag --- together with an inverse theorem: every multiplicity function on $\{0,\dots,9\}$ is realized as the lag-$1$ interval distribution of an explicit decimal melody, and every lag $\ell\ge1$ realizes $\ell N$ on a window of length $\ell n$. On the temporal side we introduce the lag spectrum $M_x(\ell)=\sup_i|x_i-x_{i+\ell}|$, prove it is a tropical (min-plus) seminorm on the lag monoid, and prove that its kernel --- the monoid of unison lags --- is closed under greatest common divisors and hence equals the set of multiples of the minimal period. The two sides are joined by a single scalar: for a cyclic digit melody, $2A(k)=2E-\sum_v v^2N_k(v)$, so autocorrelation is exactly the second moment of the lag-$k$ pitch statistic. The bridge is not invertible, as an explicit four-note example shows. We compute the exact combinatorial null model, whose second moment is $(b^4-b^2)/6$, and derive the exact deficit law $12(E-A(k))=m(b^4-b^2)$, i.e.\ $E-A=825m$ in base ten. Finally we prove that the pairwise interval matrix is idempotent in the min-plus matrix semiring, and that pitch-class reduction modulo $12$ is faithful on any alphabet of size at most $12$ and first fails in base $13$.
\end{abstract}

